## Kubernetes Services ##

> A Kubernetes Service is an abstraction that provides a stable network endpoint to access a group of Pods

>   Main jobs :- 
                provide stable ip 
                provide stable dns name
                finds pods using labels
                load-balance traffic between pods
                allows communication between applications


## How service find pods? ##

>   sewrvice-->Selector-->pod labels

> pods :

metadata:
  labels:
    app: nginx

> service :

spec:
  selector:
    app: nginx

because both are :-- app: nginx , the service sends traffic to those pods.

## Types of Services ##

> there are mainly 4 types you should konw :

| Type         | Purpose                      |
| ------------ | ---------------------------- |
| ClusterIP    | Internal access              |
| NodePort     | Expose outside the cluster   |
| LoadBalancer | External load balancer       |
| ExternalName | Maps Service to external DNS |

> Range of NodePort :- 30000-32767

*** How NodePort Works ***

let suppose:- 
                Node 1 - 192.168.1.10
                Node 2 - 192.168.1.11
                Node 3 - 192.168.1.12
     Nodeport : 30080
     then, the service can reach through :
                                            192.168.1.10:30080
                                            192.168.1.11:30080
                                            192.168.1.12:30080

even if pods are running on only one or two nodes, k8s can route traffic to the appropriate backend pods, subject to the services traffic policy.

   *** LoadBalancer ***

> LoadBalancer is commonly used when you want to expose an application externally using a cloud/provider load     balancer.

example:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: web-service

spec:
  type: LoadBalancer

  selector:
    app: web

  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080

In a cloud environment, Kubernetes asks the cloud provider to provision a load balancer.

> Examples of cloud environments that can provide load balancers include:

        AWS
        Azure
        Google Cloud
        Other Kubernetes/cloud platforms with a suitable load-balancer implementation

   *** Loadbalancer vs NodePort ***

NodePort:- Expose application using Node IP + port
LoadBalancer :- Provision/use an external load balancer

   *** Port vs TargetPort ***

port       :- Service port
targetPort :- Pod/application port   

  *** ConfigMaps ***

> A ConfigMap stores non-sensitive configuration data (like URLs, hostnames, or settings) in key-value pairs, separate from application code. It helps make applications portable and easy to manage across different environments.

> ConfigMaps can be used as:

    Environment variables
    Command-line arguments
    Mounted configuration files inside containers

 *** Secrets *** 

 > A secret in Kubernetes can be defined as an object that contains a small quantity of sensitive data like a password, a token, or a key.

>Reduces the risk of losing or exposing sensitive data during the workflow of creating, viewing, and editing Pods.
>Secrets are created independently of the Pods in which they are used.
>Secrets are similar to ConfigMaps, but are specifically designed to store confidential data.


*** ConfigMap vs Secrets ***


| Feature                   | ConfigMap                            | Secrets                                   |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------| ------------------------------------------|
| Purpose                   | Non-sensitive configuration          | Sensitive/confidential data               |
| Data examples             | App mode, URLs, ports, feature flags | Passwords, API keys tokens                |
| Data is stored as         | Plain text                           | Base64-encoded by default                 |
| Encryption by default     | No                                   | Not necessarily — Base64 is not encryption|
| Used by Pods              | Yes                                  | Yes                                       |
| Environment variables     | Yes                                  | Yes                                       |
| Volume mount              | Yes                                  | Yes                                       |
| Should contain passwords? | No                                   | Yes                                       |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|



